===============================================================================
 lmde7-fde — append the Machine Owner Key to the firmware db
 Host: {{ inventory_hostname }}
===============================================================================

WHAT THIS IS
  The factory Secure Boot hierarchy on this machine stays exactly as it
  shipped. One certificate is added to db: the key that signs this machine's
  Unified Kernel Images. Nothing is cleared and nothing is replaced.

  After the append, db trusts:
    - the board vendor's keys          (unchanged)
    - Microsoft's CAs                  (unchanged) -> Windows, and the GPU
                                                      option ROM, keep working
    - {{ mok_cn }}   <- ADDED -> the UKI boots

THE CERTIFICATE
  File   \{{ secureboot_esp_key_dir | replace('/', '\\') }}\MOK.cer
         (identical bytes are also at MOK.der, for firmware whose file
         browser filters on that extension instead)
  Subject  CN={{ mok_cn }}
  SHA-256  {{ sb_mok_fp.stdout | trim }}

  Check that fingerprint against what the firmware shows before confirming.
  It is the only thing that distinguishes the right file from a wrong one.

STEPS (ASUS wording; other vendors differ in labels, not in substance)
  1. Reboot into firmware setup.
  2. Secure Boot -> Key Management.
  3. Select "db Management" (Authorized Signatures).
  4. Choose APPEND, not Replace/Set New. Replace wipes the vendor and
     Microsoft certificates and would leave Windows and the GPU option ROM
     untrusted.
  5. Browse to the file above on this ESP and confirm.
  6. Verify the fingerprint matches the line above.
  7. Set Secure Boot to Enabled. Save and exit.

AFTER THE REBOOT
  Expect the LUKS recovery passphrase ONCE. Enabling Secure Boot changes
  PCR 7, and the TPM2 keyslot is sealed to PCR 7, so it will not unseal until
  it is re-sealed against the new value. Re-seal from the UKI you intend to
  keep booting:

      sudo ansible-playbook tpm2.yml -e target_mount=/

  Then confirm the whole picture with VERIFY.md.

IF SOMETHING WILL NOT BOOT
  Turn Secure Boot off in firmware setup. Everything boots again, including
  the shim/GRUB entries. Nothing in this procedure is one-way: the appended
  certificate can be deleted from db in the same menu, and "Restore factory
  keys" puts db back exactly as it shipped.

